            "content": [
              {
                "text": "I'm trying to figure out the tiling rule from example 4: a 2x4 grid with a colored cell gets tiled 3x3 into 6x12, where the row containing the colored cell fills with 1s except at the color's position, while I'm still checking what happens to the non-colored rows.\n\nChecking example 2 (2x3 input, colored cell) confirms the same pattern: the colored row alternates 1s around the color, while the other rows only show a 3 at column 0, not at both diagonal positions as I'd expect\u2014so the 3s aren't simple diagonal markers from every colored cell, something more specific is going on.\n\nLooking at example 1 (4x3 input, 9 at position 0,2), I trace how the 3s appear at column 3 and column 6 in row1, which are one column right of each tile's 9 position\u2014suggesting the 3 marks the down-right diagonal from the 9 specifically, tile by tile.\n\nNow checking row3, the 3s appear one column left of the 9's position, matching the up-left diagonal from the next row's 9. So the pattern seems to be: draw down-right diagonal and up-left diagonal from each 9, nothing else. I verify this against example 2 with the 4 at (1,1)\u2014both diagonals check out consistently, and example 4's row2 also fits the up-left pattern.\n\nI confirm this on example 3 with the 8s at (0,1) and (0,3): the row below shows 3s down-right, the row above shows 3s up-left, and rows without adjacent 9s stay all zero. Colored rows keep their color otherwise turn to 1, with 3s marking diagonal cells crossing tile boundaries using global coordinates. I'm ready to apply this to the test input.\n\n",
